The CIS Critical Security Controls
----------------------------------

src/cis_controls/schema/*.yaml, src/cis_controls/datamodel/, project/ and
src/cis_controls/data/cis-controls-8.1.2.yaml model the structure of the
CIS Critical Security Controls (CIS Controls), Version 8.1, as the Center
for Internet Security, Inc. (CIS) revised it in March 2025, when the
guide's pages were labelled v8.1.2: https://www.cisecurity.org/controls/v8-1,
read on 2026-10-30. They are this project's work, written in its own
words. They are not CIS's, and their use does not imply endorsement by
CIS. CIS Critical Security Controls and CIS Controls are registered
trademarks of CIS, named here only to say which framework is modelled.

CIS licenses the CIS Controls under the Creative Commons
Attribution-NonCommercial-NoDerivatives 4.0 International licence
(CC BY-NC-ND 4.0, https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/).
That licence lets anyone copy and share CIS's guide unchanged, for
non-commercial purposes, with credit to CIS and a link to the licence. It
does not let anyone share a changed or adapted version, and CIS requires
its prior approval for any commercial use. So this repository carries no
text of CIS's: no title, description, overview, explanation or procedure
from the guide, copied or paraphrased.

What the repository carries is the structure the guide sets out, which is
fact, and this project's own words about it. The edition file,
src/cis_controls/data/cis-controls-8.1.2.yaml, names each Control and
Safeguard by its CIS number, records which Safeguards belong to which
Control and each Safeguard's asset class, security function and
Implementation Groups, and gives every Control and Safeguard a title and
a description that this project wrote. Each description was written from
what the Control or Safeguard requires, in one or two sentences, not by
rewording CIS's sentences; each title is this project's own name for the
element, never CIS's. Copyright protects the way CIS expressed the
Controls, not the methods, procedures and facts they describe, and CC
BY-NC-ND 4.0 restricts nothing that copyright does not (its section 8(a)).
The project nevertheless takes the stricter reading that European law
allows, under which even a short extract can be protected when it shows
its author's own choices (Court of Justice of the European Union, Infopaq
International, C-5/08), so it reuses no title or phrase of CIS's. CIS's
guide remains the authority on what the CIS Controls say; this project's
words describe the Controls and do not replace them.

The schema's text slots, title and description, hold whatever their user
writes into them. Fill them only with text you have the right to use: your
own words, this project's under its licence, or CIS's text under CIS's
licence and any approval CIS has given you. The Apache License of this
repository does not cover CIS's text.

